›ULDG installation                                         Help›u
 
The installer copy files in your system folders in order to 
LDG system be  ready to use after the  next reboot. The
installer is divided  into 3 sections. To activate a section
installation you have to check-in its button title.

To edit a field, use the keyboard arrows and type the directory
pathname or click directly on a field to use the system
file selector.

›USystem section›u : install or update LDG into your system.
 
    ›BAUTO-path›b  : your TSR directory, usually C:\AUTO
    ›BCPX-path›b   : your CPX directory, usually C:\CPX
    ›BSTART-path›b : Auto Start application (for multitasking 
                 system)
    ›BLDG-path›b   : the directory where LDG-lib are stored,
                 usually C:\GEMSYS\LDG,
    ›BHYP-path›b   : the directory where ST-Guide files are 
                 stored.
    ›BExtras›b     : contribution and utility programs. 
 
›UDevelopper section›u : install C-libraries needed to create new
                     LDG libraries or LDG applications. 

    ›BInclude›b    : header files for C compilers
    ›BLib›b        : libraries for C compilers
    ›BGcc, Gcc 295, Pure C, Sozobon X›b: select your favorite 
                 compiler
    
    Gcc 295 designs the last version of Gcc available for 
    our computers. However, gcc-2.9.5 works only in a MiNT 
    framework with an Unix-like hierarchy. Interested people 
    will recognize themselves.

    Examples  of  library  and  application using LDG will be 
    copied in the directory Extras of the previous section. 
  
›UUnix tools›u : if you use an Un*x-like hierachy  and  a  shell, 
			 this section proposes some interesting tools.

    ›BBin›b        : path of unix programs
    ›BMan pages›b  : path of unix manual pages (man)

Notes : If a subfolder don't exist, it will be created.
 
Fill in correctly each fields and select button 'Install'  to
start the installation.
›U                                                             ›u

